On Friday, July 19, 2013 11:15:32 PM UTC-4, Carlos wrote:

> > But in your case, A is diagonal so the log determinant is just sum(log(A1)) which is much faster than doing anything on an arbitrary positive definite matrix.
>

> Mmmmm nope, A is not diagonal. I create first an identity matrix, but then I assign also some values in the off-diagonal cells. The idea is that A in general is not diagonal. The off-diagonal values represent the restrictions on the contemporaneous relationships among the variables in y.

I said the wrong thing. In your example,

    // Build matrix A
    A           <- diag_matrix(rep_vector(1, 3));
    A[2,1]      <- A1[1];
    A[3,1]      <- A1[2];
    A[3,2]      <- A1[3];

A is lower-triangular, in which case its log determinant is still 2 * sum(log(A1)). So, I was wrong to say that it was diagonal but the expression for its log determinant is the same as if it were diagonal. But if in your real problem, A is not triangular, then go ahead and use Marcus' log_determinant() function.

Ben

