> What you've done here is to essentially make the problem even worse.  By decreasing the maximum tree depth and lowering the target acceptance rate you're inducing random walk behavior that drastically slows the exploration of the sampler.  This keeps the chain in a small neighborhood where the covariance is approximately constant and the adaptation appears to be working fine.

This is a good point. I tried this approach in another problem and the maximum tree depth was too small, creating small effective sample sizes. I've also tried only limiting the maximum tree depth in the initial run while leaving it higher (or even the default) in the full run. This seems to work well, but again has issues with poor convergence diagnostics because of the same initial starting values.

Up to this point it seems like lowering max tree depth does the most to help get my chains to finish at around the same time, even when using random starts to help maintain convergence diagnostics. Due to the issue you mention, how low is too low for max tree depth? What makes 2^10 ideal? Shouldn't I be able to get away with 2^1 and simply have to run for a really long time (since it is closest to a random walk)? Won't effective sample sizes illustrate this to me, or is it possible for me to be fooled without using a very high max tree depth?

