Thanks Bob.
Re my case, just looking to use sorted predicted values (and associated index) to help model heteroscedasticity in a variance term.  There are other approaches to address this case, but it would be helpful to have the same sorting functionality available in R for other uses.

On Tuesday, July 23, 2013 12:16:11 AM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> Yes, Marco's sort and rank implementations have been
> merged into our develop branch.   But they won't be in
> an official release until 2.0 comes out, which we are
> narrowing in on.
>
> There's nothing returning the array of indices in
> sort order, but I can see where that would be useful.
>
> Could I inquire as to the use case?  It'll help motivate
> us to do it and give us an example to write up.
>
> In C++, we'd just use a pair consisting of the index
> and value, sort the pairs on value, then dump out the
> indices.
>
> - Bob
>
> On 7/22/13 6:16 PM, Richard Alexander wrote:
> > Just checking ot see if a sort routine is available now, and one that possibly includes an index item as that in the R
> > sort routine.
> > Thanks.
> >
> > On Thursday, May 2, 2013 2:09:57 AM UTC-4, Marco Inacio wrote:
> >
> >     I pushed sort and rank functions to my fork now.
> >
> >     If I have enough time, tomorrow I'll test and add them to the reference
> >     manual (as well as the float point sum precision functions) and then
> >     create a pull request.
> >
> >      > There's a section on sorting and interpolation.
> >      > But you're right --- sort() itself isn't on the list!
> >      >
